  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Strong high pressure building into the Mid-Atlantic \nand low pressure well off the SE coast will result in a period of \nbreezy to strong NE winds returning tonight through early next week \nwith strongest winds along the coast, especially south of Cape \nHatteras where gradients will be tightest. This, combined with \nupcoming King Tide cycle could lead to additional minor coastal \nimpacts (coastal flooding and rough surf).\n\nFor Hatteras Island, long period swells have subsided slightly to \naround 6-8 ft, however with increasing NE winds, seas are expected \nto build up to 7-9 ft tonight and continue through the weekend. This \nwill continue to bring the threat of ocean overwash to vulnerable \nareas where the dune structure has been compromised, especially \naround the times of high tide. For ocean-side: tonight's high tide \nis around 6 pm this evening and between 6 and 7 AM Saturday. \nSoundside high tides range from 2 to 3 hours behind the oceanside \ntides. The National Parks Service and NCDOT have reported \nsubstantial beach erosion from south Nags Head to Buxton, along with \nsignificant overwash occurring on side streets in Rodanthe. Due to \nhazardous road conditions and flooding, NC-12 remains closed from \nthe Marc Basnight Bridge to Rodanthe. \n\nThe region is also entering a King Tide cycle this weekend that will \ncontinue through next week. Expect higher than normal tide cycles \nwith minor \"sunny day\" coastal flooding across much of the tidal \nregions of ENC around times of high tide. The areas most susceptible \nto street flooding will be low lying areas along the Neuse and Bay \nrivers as well as Downeast Carteret County to North Topsail Beach as \npersistent moderate NE winds will contribute to additional waters \nrises. The morning high tide will be the highest of the daily tides \nthis weekend and next week with the King Tide peaking TUE and WED.\n\nBecause of all this and the minor localized flooding that was \nalready seen with this morning's high tide, have extended the pre-\nexisting Coastal Flood Advisory for Hatteras Island through the \nremainder of the weekend. Have also issued new coastal flood \nadvisories starting with tomorrow morning's high tide for Northern \nOuter Banks, Ocracoke, the entire Crystal Coast, and the areas \nadjacent to Neuse and Bay Rivers (Southern Craven and Pamlico \ncounties) lasting through Monday morning's high tide. As stated \nabove, the King Tide will peak TUE and WED, so these advisories will \nlikely need to be extended from their current end times, but didn't \nwant to to go out that far just yet to see if just an extension will \nsuffice or if a Coastal Flood Warning would be warranted for the \nMON-WED timeframe. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Pleasantly cooler air mass will remain with us \nthrough the next several days with temps continuing near to slightly \nbelow climo. Maximum temperatures through the period are expected to \nremain in the mid 60s to lower 70s range. \n\nGood radiational cooling conditions last night led to lows in the \nlow to mid 30s across the bulk of the FA. Slightly more cloud \ncoverage tonight and NE winds will bring light mixing during the \novernight hours tonight through the weekend, keeping temps from \ndropping as much as the previous two nights. Generally expect \novernight lows from the mid 40s inland to the 50s to around 60 along \nthe coast. These temps and winds should preclude any frost \ndevelopment.",
